    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p3_b6",
      "text": "A natural alternative is to treat the reward model as an uncertain objective and import\nrobust optimization. Standard distributionally robust optimization (DRO) evaluates a\npolicy by its worst plausible reward model inside an ambiguity set around the learned\nreward model, drawing on a powerful operations research toolkit for robust and Wasser-\nstein distributionally robust optimization [Ben-Tal et al., 2013, Wiesemann et al., 2014,\nMohajerin Esfahani and Kuhn, 2018]. In our setting, the Wasserstein distance is taken\nover reward laws, and the transport cost is the promptwise \u2113p distance between reward\nvectors.\n",
      "bbox": [
        71.7,
        556.8,
        525.31,
        694.15
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
        286,
        2227,
        2101,
        2776
      ],
      "coord_space": "pdf_points",
      "font_size": 11.84,
      "font_name": "LMRoman12-Regular",
      "is_math": false,
      "char_count": 616,
      "block_type": 0
    },
    {
      "block_id": "2605.00155_p3_b7",
      "text": "Yet worst-case value is not always the right benchmark. Pessimistic value penalties\nand restrictive feasible regions can become over-conservative [Xu et al., 2025, Li et al.,\n",
      "bbox": [
        72.0,
        700.04,
        524.96,
        730.03
      ],
      "rendered_bbox": [
